      "category": "path-traversal",
      "severity": "CRITICAL",
      "files": ["src/files.js", "src/storage.js"],
      "evidence": "User-controlled URL-encoded document name flows from req.query.name -> decodeURIComponent(req.query.name) in src/files.js:3 (download) into readDocument(name) -> fs.readFileSync(path.join(ROOT, name)) in src/storage.js:5. path.join('/srv/documents', name) DOES canonicalize '..' segments, but no containment check follows, so the canonicalized result escapes ROOT. Verified at runtime: path.join('/srv/documents', '../../etc/passwd') returns '/etc/passwd' (path.resolve agrees), and that string does NOT start with '/srv/documents' (contained?=false). A request like GET /download?name=..%2F..%2F..%2Fetc%2Fpasswd therefore yields fs.readFileSync('/etc/passwd') and the bytes are returned via res.send(), giving arbitrary file read of any path readable by the Node process. The decodeURIComponent step additionally enables double-decode bypasses (e.g. %252F -> %2F after the query parser -> / after decodeURIComponent), but traversal is exploitable even with plain '../' since name is concatenated into the path with no allow-listing or prefix check.",
      "remediation": "Canonicalize and enforce containment before any fs access. In storage.js compute the absolute target with path.resolve(ROOT, name) (or path.join(ROOT, name)) and reject unless resolved === ROOT || resolved.startsWith(ROOT + path.sep). Better, validate name against an allow-list of expected characters (e.g. /^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$/) and reject any path separators or '..' segments before joining. Do NOT rely on path.join alone to contain traversal, and avoid the redundant decodeURIComponent (let the query parser's single decode stand) to remove the double-decode amplification surface."
    }
  ],
  "scanned_files": ["src/files.js", "src/storage.js"],
  "source_modified": false
}
"}

